WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.127
DELETE physically removes rows, so we need to distinguish it from a soft-delete pattern.

00:00:05.127 --> 00:00:09.277
A soft delete keeps the row but changes a status field such as is active.

00:00:09.277 --> 00:00:14.052
That can preserve history while excluding the record from normal active views.

00:00:14.052 --> 00:00:17.002
A hard delete removes the row from the table.

00:00:17.002 --> 00:00:20.872
Run the soft-delete example and inspect the row after the change.

00:00:20.872 --> 00:00:23.322
Notice that the patient still exists but the active flag has changed.

00:00:23.322 --> 00:00:30.732
Whether a system should use soft or hard deletion depends on retention requirements, audit needs,

00:00:30.732 --> 00:00:34.982
and business rules. Before any hard delete, preview the exact target rows.

00:00:34.982 --> 00:00:40.617
Also remember that foreign-key constraints may block a deletion when related records depend on that row.

00:00:40.617 --> 00:00:42.362
Writes deserve a slower workflow than reads.

00:00:42.362 --> 00:00:48.707
Before UPDATE or DELETE, run the intended WHERE condition as a SELECT and inspect the affected rows.

00:00:48.707 --> 00:00:54.127
In production, use transactions when appropriate so you can verify the change before committing.

00:00:54.127 --> 00:00:58.000
The absence of a syntax error does not mean the correct records were modified.
